        "<strong>Indicator grading scale</strong><ul class=\"bulletList\" style=\"margin-top:6px\"><li><strong>1</strong> – Minimal support (Severe Deficiency)</li><li><strong>2</strong> – Basic support (Moderate Deficiency)</li><li><strong>3</strong> – Moderate support (Developing)</li><li><strong>4</strong> – Strong support (Effective)</li><li><strong>5</strong> – Exemplary support (Leading Practice)</li></ul>",
        "WA's research and development policy for the zero-emissions transition is scored as a 4, holding the Strong Support rating recorded in the Kwinana Regional Readiness Report (May 2026).",
        "WA's R&D suite sits under the \"Diversifying WA\" framework<sup class=\"refMark\">1</sup> and the Future State prospectus (ref - link broken), anchored by the WA Innovation Strategy and the WA Hydrogen Strategy. The $1 billion Strategic Industries Fund<sup class=\"refMark\">2</sup> continues to underwrite both greenfield and brownfield strategic industrial areas (SIAs) across the state. Most significantly for this report, the 2026–27 State Budget (handed down 7 May 2026) allocated $27.1 million for planning and design of the Boodarie SIA — the strategic industrial area directly adjacent to Port Hedland — alongside $23.8 million to investigate water supply options for the site<sup class=\"refMark\">3</sup>, within a wider $91.7–92 million Strategic Industries Fund tranche covering the Western Trade Coast, Kemerton, and Boodarie SIAs<sup class=\"refMark\">4</sup>. The same Budget committed $659 million to expand the Dampier Seawater Desalination Plant connected to the West Pilbara Water Supply Scheme, and $50 million toward the Port of Port Hedland's Channel Zone 5 Bypass<sup class=\"refMark\">5</sup>.",
        "A visible delivery milestone landed directly in Port Hedland in March 2026, with completion of the Great Northern Highway Lumsden Interchange<sup class=\"refMark\">6</sup> — common-user road infrastructure supporting the broader, state-funded Pilbara Hydrogen Hub and its planned Clean Energy Training and Research Institute (CETRI), linked to the Burrup SIA<sup class=\"refMark\">7</sup>. The Hub carries $70 million in state funding, matched by a further $70 million federally<sup class=\"refMark\">8</sup>, but CETRI itself remains pre-operational, with first intake not expected until 2027.",
        "Industry-research collaboration in the Pilbara has deepened since the KRRR. The \"Made in the Pilbara\" Grant Program — combining the WA Government's Local Manufacturing Investment Fund with BHP co-investment, delivered through the CORE Innovation Hub — funded 11 Pilbara businesses in February 2026<sup class=\"refMark\">9</sup> across Small Grants (up to $50,000) and Innovation Seed Funding (up to $25,000) streams, projected to create more than 40 jobs and 14 apprenticeships and unlock over $800,000 in further private investment<sup class=\"refMark\">10</sup>. Round 2 of the $15 million Carbon Innovation Grants Program, results announced in December 2025, delivered two Pilbara-anchored projects: a $1.5 million pilot plant for Airbridge Pty Ltd to capture and utilise carbon dioxide at Yara Pilbara Fertilisers' Burrup Peninsula facility, targeting at least 2,000 tonnes of CO2 captured annually<sup class=\"refMark\">11</sup>, and a $250,000 feasibility study for Australian Flow Batteries Pty Ltd into a megawatt-scale vanadium flow battery manufacturing facility in the Pilbara or Kalgoorlie. The Clean Energy Future Fund's fourth round, $9 million across grants of $100,000–$4 million prioritising First Nations and green export benefits, closed in April 2026 with recipients due late 2026<sup class=\"refMark\">12</sup>. Other non-exclusive state R&D funds — the New Industries and Innovation Fund ($40 million), Research and Capability funding ($44 million)<sup class=\"refMark\">13</sup>, and TAFE applied research funding ($1.5 million, with a First Nations focus)<sup class=\"refMark\">14</sup> — remain available to Pilbara applicants on the same statewide competitive basis as elsewhere in WA.",
        "The score remains short of Exemplary for the same reasons identified in the KRRR. No dedicated, ring-fenced Pilbara R&D funding stream exists; every program above is a competitive statewide fund. CETRI will not take its first intake until 2027. No formal, published evaluation framework exists for WA's R&D programs collectively, and substantive international collaboration remains limited to the Mid West's Trilateral Hydrogen Hub Feasibility Study with Germany and the Netherlands<sup class=\"refMark\">15</sup>, which has no Pilbara equivalent. To move toward Exemplary Support, the WA Government would need to establish a Pilbara-specific R&D funding allocation, accelerate CETRI toward operational status, and introduce a published evaluation and adaptation mechanism for its R&D programs as a group.",
