        "<strong>Indicator grading scale</strong><ul class=\"bulletList\" style=\"margin-top:6px\"><li><strong>1</strong> – Minimal level of planning (Severe Deficiency)</li><li><strong>2</strong> – Basic level of planning (Moderate Deficiency)</li><li><strong>3</strong> – Moderate level of planning (Developing)</li><li><strong>4</strong> – Strong level of planning (Effective)</li><li><strong>5</strong> – Exemplary level of planning (Leading Practice)</li></ul>",
        "Strategic and nature-positive land-use planning scores a 2. Public data exists for biodiversity and agriculture, but is generally only available as printable maps rather than downloadable GIS datasets. For the Pilbara region, limited data is available on the energy infrastructure, because privately owned infrastructure data is not readily available. No combined biodiversity prioritisation layer has been identified, and while research on strategic land use covers Perth and Peel, no similar assessment has been done for regions in the Pilbara. A bilateral agreement between the Federal and Western Australian governments on coordinating EPBC approvals is pending until the end of 2026. Western Australia has a planning framework and regional planning effort underway, but lacks a single, spatially-explicit, cross-sectoral land-use plan.",
        "Data WA is a key repository of freely available and pay-for-access GIS data sets, which includes directions to relevant departments to request data access where required. Different components of biodiversity data are generated by different government departments. For example the Department of Biodiversity, Conservation and Attractions is the point of contact for access to threatened species and communities data, https://catalogue.data.wa.gov.au/dataset/threatened-ecological-communities, however a combined prioritised biodiversity layer would more likely come from Department of Water and Environmental Regulation (DWER) and strategic land use planning would be undertaken by Department of Planning, Lands and Heritage. DWER has an online data sharing platform, Environment Online https://environmentonline.dwer.wa.gov.au/interactive-map/ for printable maps, with many data sets in common with Landgate (Western Australia’s land information authority’s (Landgate’s) Shared Location Information Platform (SLIP) mapping website https://maps.slip.wa.gov.au/landgate/locate/. A free community data website exists called Dandjoo (https://bio.wa.gov.au/dandjoo) but includes very limited data for the collated threatened flora and fauna data points, with a large number of other data sets available that would require expertise to collate meaningfully into a biodiversity prioritisation layer. Data relating to prioritisation of quality agricultural land is available via DIPRD’s Natural Resource Information (NRInfo) as printable maps. (https://www.dpird.wa.gov.au/environment-and-sustainability/nrinfo-for-western-australia/), however some key data sets are behind paywalls such as designated agricultural areas https://catalogue.data.wa.gov.au/dataset/agricultural-areas.",
        "Energy system data for WA and Port Hedland / the Pilbara is not readily available from the state government but is available via the Open Electricity data project (https://openelectricity.org.au/facilities). However, for the Pilbara and Port Hedland, only the transmission network data is available, which is generated nationally from publicly available publications from utility companies, engineering firms and government agencies. https://digital.atlas.gov.au/datasets/digitalatlas::electricity-transmission-lines/about. Renewable energy generation that is run privately such as on mining leases appears not to be available in the Pilbara region. Our investigation has not found freely, publicly available sources of collated energy generation GIS data for the region. While BZE has a licence to use the RenewMaps paid platform for our National Action Plan assessments, this highlights an issue for the public and researchers working on strategic land use planning.",
        "The news of an MoU between the Western Australian Government and the Federal government to develop a bilateral agreement to coordinate and collaborate on EPBC approvals by the end of this year<sup class=\"refMark\">1</sup> is a welcome step towards greater resourcing and attention towards biodiversity protection and strategic land use planning for renewable energy and other infrastructure project approvals. However the bilateral agreement is not due until the end of this year. How it works is yet to be seen. The public conversation to build consensus around strategic land use planning will also need to be elevated. WA has a planning framework in place and a substantial integrated regional planning effort underway. However, it doesn't yet have a single, spatially-explicit, cross-sectoral land-use plan. WAs State Planning Strategy 2050<sup class=\"refMark\">2</sup> provides strategic context for all planning and development decisions in WA, structured around six principles (Community, Economy, Environment, Infrastructure, Regional Development, and Governance). It's high-level and sets direction, but is not an integrated plan.",
        "Under the State Planning Strategy 2050<sup class=\"refMark\">2</sup> sits the Western Australian Planning Commission’s (WAPC's) state planning policy framework. As part of this framework, in June 2022 the WAPC authorised preparation of eight new Regional Planning Strategies covering Gascoyne, Goldfields-Esperance, Great Southern, Kimberley, Mid West, Pilbara, South West and Wheatbelt.These \"20-year integrated regional plans to establish the land use, infrastructure and environmental needs of each region\"<sup class=\"refMark\">3</sup>. However, the publicly available material is dated and there is no evidence that any of the eight have been published as final documents.",
        "Two other planning instruments of note are the Draft Renewable Energy Planning Code (DREP)<sup class=\"refMark\">4</sup> and Infrastructure WA's State Infrastructure Strategy 2022<sup class=\"refMark\">5</sup>. The DREP was released by the WAPC for consultation from 12 December 2025 to 10 April 2026, with accompanying Guidelines. It is an assessment code, not a strategic siting product. Additionally, WA does not have a Renewable Energy Zone (REZ) framework.",
        "Infrastructure WA's State Infrastructure Strategy 2022<sup class=\"refMark\">5</sup> addresses infrastructure needs rather than land-use planning for nature-positive land use. It includes regional sections (Wheatbelt and Pilbara have their own treatments) and identifies cross-cutting themes like regional development and planning coordination. Community and industry engagement is indicated to be happening around the unreleased refreshed Regional Planning Strategies, the State Infrastructure Strategy 2022 statutory 5-year refresh<sup class=\"refMark\">5</sup>, as well as the WAPC's draft Renewable Energy Planning Code and Guidelines due for incorporation into local planning schemes late 2026<sup class=\"refMark\">4</sup>. However this consultation is broad, across departments, focused on reports and principles but not specifically focused on nature-positive strategic land use planning to enable best placement of renewable energy projects.",
